00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:12.000
I flew West. I followed the edge of the desert, trying to find a place to cross. But it was endless. 

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
The golden dunes were laughing at me, teasing me. 

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
“Do you really think you can escape us? Do you really believe no one thought of this before? 

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Face your fear, little bird. There is no way around us.” 

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:43.000
But I couldn’t face it. Not yet. That desert was too big. You couldn’t see the end of it, the sand merged with the sky in the distance, blending in infinity. 

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:50.000
“Come on, little bird, called the dunes. The dream is on the other side.” 

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.000
I flew for days, refusing to give up. There must be a better way. 

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.000
And then, I reached the ocean. Another barrier. 

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:12.000
The Atlantic faced me with all its power, huge waves crashing on the rocks, threatening to blow me to pieces. 

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.000
In the distance I saw a beach, hedged with coconut trees. I landed in one of them. 

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.000
After days of helplessly flying over deserted plains, the lush leaves felt like a soft blanket against my body. 

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:45.000
I was so exhausted that I fell asleep almost immediately.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.000
I woke up a few hours later to a horrible cacophony. 

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
There were birds perched in all the surrounding trees, hundreds of them, singing and chittering all around me. 

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.000
After those weeks of lonely travel, I couldn’t face the shock. 

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
“What is this place?” I muttered to myself. 

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.000
“Welcome to paradise!” Shouted a big red bird, perched on the next branch. 

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
“The tropical haven for gutless birds of all horizons.”   

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
“Why are you saying that?” I asked, defensive. 

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.000
“Isn’t it why you are here? This is where all migrants who can’t face the big bad desert end up. 

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Some give up. Some eat to find the courage. Some drink to forget they have none. 

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Would you like some coconut juice? The sugar will sweep your worries away.”

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.000
I ignored him. 

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
 “Suit yourself, spoiler”, said the loud bird, and he flew away. 


00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:57.000
I looked at the crowd. It was a party of birds from across the continent. They were travellers like me, and yet, I felt so alien. 

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Maybe that red bird was right. Maybe I was just too scared to cross. 

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Then suddenly I spotted a tiny green dot on a rock by the sea. 

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.000
It was another willow warbler, staring at the waves in silence. 

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.000
I approached him. He was probably my age, but there was something different about him. 

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.000
He looked stronger. His feathers looked longer, sharper. His eyes were more determined. 

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:33.000
“So you are also figuring things out, right?”, I said, to break the ice. 

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:43.000
“There is nothing to figure out”, he shrugged. “You just need to get ready. I’m flying tomorrow. Clearly, you haven’t started yet.” 

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.000
“Started what?” I asked 

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.000
“Moulting. Growing new feathers for the crossing. How else would you expect to do this?”

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.000
“But I thought we were only moulting after the journey…” I muttered

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.000
“Not us. We are willow warblers. How do you think small birds like us manage to cross the Sahara?

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Nature knew she had to give us a hand. Look at my new feathers. Sharp, aren’t they? It’s time to get rid of yours.”

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.000
“But how?” I wondered “I’ve had these feathers since I was born.” 

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:28.000
“Oh my darling, you’ll have to go through much bigger changes than this. You’re a migrant now. You’ll adapt. It’s our only way to survive.”  

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:37.000
So I did. I changed. It took me two weeks. I remember weeping when I saw my first feather fall. 

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:43.000
I looked at my reflection in puddles and barely recognised myself. 

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:52.000
But alongside my feathers fell the fear. My confidence grew, with every new feather I became stronger.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.000
And then, the day came. And I was ready. 
